e there\'s a way to optimize. However, for the purposes of solving the problem, the standard approach is sufficient.\n\nSo, the code would look something like this:\n\ndef lps(s):\n    n = len(s)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n-1, -1, -1):\n        for j in range(i, n):\n            if i == j:\n                dp[i][j] = 1\n            elif s[i] == s[j]:\n                dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i+1][j-1] if j > i+1 else 2\n            else:\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i+1][j], dp[i][j-1])\n    return dp[0][n-1]\n\nWait, but when i and j are adjacent, like i = j-1, then j > i+1 is false, so the code would add 2. But when j = i+1, the code checks if j > i+1, which is false, so it just takes 2. That\'s correct because if the two characters are the same, the LPS is 2.\n\nBut in the code above, when j > i+1, the code adds 2. But for j = i+1, the code would do 2 + dp[i+1][j-1], which is 2 + dp[i+1][i], but since i+1 > i, that\'s 2 + 0 (since i+1 and i are adjacent). Wait, no, when i and j are adjacent, j = i+1. Then j > i+1 is false, so the code takes max(dp[i+1][j], dp[i][j-1]). But dp[i+1][j] is dp[i+1][i+1], which is 1, and dp[i][j-1] is dp[i][i], which is 1. So max(1,1) is 1, and adding 2? Wait no, the code is:\n\nif s[i] == s[j]:\n    dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i+1][j-1] if j > i+1 else 2\n\nWait, when j > i+1, then dp[i+1][j-1] is computed. But when j = i+1, then j > i+1 is false, so it just returns 2. That\'s correct because if the two characters are the same, the LPS is 2.\n\nSo the code should work.\n\nBut maybe there\'s a more efficient way. However, the problem doesn\'t specify any constraints on the input size, so the standard approach is acceptable.\n\nNow, the code needs to be in experiments/solution.py. So the code would be:\n\ndef lps(s):\n    n = len(s)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n-1, -1, -1):\n        for j in range(i, n):\n            i